{
  "requirement": "`habeas::validate_repository(root)` MUST return a report with the number of discovered requirements, the number of evidence files, and all validation errors for the supplied project. Ordinary invalid evidence MUST be reported in that report; an inaccessible project root MUST return an I/O error.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The implementation looks right, but no test covers the I/O-error branch of the requirement.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `validate_repository` (`src/evidence.rs:119`) delegates to `inspect_repository`. That calls `inspect_repository_with_progress`, which begins with `fs::canonicalize(root)?`, so a missing or inaccessible root returns `Err(io::Error)`.\n- `EvidenceReport` carries `requirements`, `evidence_files` and `errors`. The function pushes invalid JSON, schema problems, orphaned files, missing evidence and stale inputs into `errors` instead of failing the call.\n- The `{impl}` markings on the struct and these three functions cover the behavior.\n- `EvidenceReport` also has an `examples` count that this requirement does not mention. It is probably justified by the examples requirements, so I do not count it as a gap.\n\n**Tests**\n- Many `{test}` sites exercise the success and report paths. Examples are `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines`, which asserts the requirement and evidence-file counts, and `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors`, which asserts that many kinds of invalid evidence appear in `report.errors` while the call still returns `Ok`.\n- Other tests assert the counts or the error list in the same way: `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase`, `fixme_fixture` and `discovered_external_cargo_outputs_validate_freshness_and_obey_disallow`.\n- No test calls `validate_repository` on a nonexistent or unreadable root and asserts that it returns `Err`. I searched `src` and `tests` for `NotFound`, \"nonexistent\", \"inaccessible\" and similar terms. Every `validate_repository` call in the references output is followed by `.unwrap()` or `.unwrap().errors`.\n- The requirement says \"an inaccessible project root MUST return an I/O error\". That clause is untested, so a regression such as folding the failure into `errors` would go unnoticed. The test is trivial to write and nothing makes it impractical.\n\nNo `FIXME` comment acknowledges the gap, so the judgment is `false`, not a `FIXME`.",
  "inputs": [
